<p>
  "How long will my kitchen be torn apart?" is one of the first questions Plano homeowners ask. The honest answer: a full kitchen remodel in our area typically takes <strong>six to twelve weeks</strong>, and the exact number depends far more on decisions you make up front than on the work itself.
</p>

<h2>The phases of a Plano kitchen remodel</h2>
<h3>Weeks 1–3: Design and selections</h3>
<p>
  Before demolition begins, the design and material selections are finalized. This is where the timeline is truly won or lost. Cabinets, countertops, and tile that are fully specified before construction starts keep the job moving; making choices mid-build is the most common cause of delays. Our <a href="/our-process/">design-build process</a> handles this phase in-house so nothing stalls between steps.
</p>
<h3>Weeks 2–4: Permits and approvals</h3>
<p>
  Kitchen remodels that alter walls or relocate plumbing and electrical typically require permits with the City of Plano. Permitting adds a little lead time up front but protects you and your home. In neighborhoods with an HOA, some communities also want approval for work that affects the home's exterior or structural appearance.
</p>
<h3>Weeks 4–5: Demo and rough-in</h3>
<p>
  The old kitchen comes out, and new plumbing, electrical, and any structural work go in. Walls, ceiling, and subfloor prep are completed before anything you can see is installed.
</p>
<h3>Weeks 6–9: Installation</h3>
<p>
  Cabinets go in first, followed by countertops, backsplash, and flooring. Trim, doors, and lighting finish out the space. This is the longest stretch of the project and where the quality of the install team shows.
</p>
<h3>Weeks 10–12: Final finishes and punch list</h3>
<p>
  The last few weeks cover final adjustments, touch-ups, appliance hookups, and a detailed walkthrough. A clean punch list is the difference between a remodel that ends smoothly and one that drags on.
</p>

<h2>What makes a kitchen remodel take longer?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Structural changes</strong> — removing a load-bearing wall or adding an island with new plumbing and electrical.</li>
  <li><strong>Relocating plumbing</strong> — moving the sink or dishwasher across the room.</li>
  <li><strong>Custom or specialty materials</strong> — longer fabrication lead times for custom cabinets or specialty stone.</li>
  <li><strong>Undecided selections</strong> — by far the most avoidable delay.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What keeps it on schedule</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Finalizing all material selections before demo starts.</li>
  <li>Working with one team that handles design and construction together, so nothing falls between vendors.</li>
  <li>Keeping the layout within the existing footprint when possible — <a href="/plano-kitchen-remodeling/">Plano kitchen remodels</a> that reuse the existing footprint and plumbing run faster and cost less.</li>
</ul>

